Microsoft Learn: Introduction to Azure Network Watcher

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Introduction to Azure Network Watcher
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Beginner
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn what Azure Network Watcher is, how it helps monitor and troubleshoot Azure network infrastructure, and when it should be used to diagnose connectivity, routing, security, and performance issues.

This module focused on understanding Azure Network Watcher capabilities, how its monitoring and diagnostic tools work, and how Azure Administrators can use it to maintain healthy and secure network environments.

Step 1: Understanding Azure Network Watcher

The module introduced Azure Network Watcher as Azure's built-in network monitoring and diagnostics service.

Azure Network Watcher helps administrators:

Monitor network performance
Diagnose connectivity issues
Analyze traffic flow
Troubleshoot routing problems
Validate security configurations
Monitor network resources
Key benefits:
Network visibility
Faster troubleshooting
Performance monitoring
Security validation
Reduced downtime

The module emphasized that Network Watcher is designed specifically for Azure networking diagnostics.

Step 2: Understanding the Purpose of Network Watcher

The training focused on why organizations use Network Watcher.

Common networking problems:
Virtual machines cannot communicate
Applications are unreachable
Network Security Group rules block traffic
Routing issues prevent connectivity
High network latency
Unexpected traffic behavior
Administrative goals:
Quickly identify root causes
Reduce troubleshooting time
Improve network reliability
Validate network configurations

Network Watcher provides centralized tools to investigate these issues.

Step 3: Understanding How Azure Network Watcher Works

The module introduced several built-in diagnostic tools available through Network Watcher.

Key capabilities:
Capability	Purpose
Connectivity Monitoring	Verify communication between resources
Traffic Analysis	Analyze network traffic patterns
Route Diagnostics	Validate routing behavior
Security Validation	Verify NSG configurations
Packet Capture	Capture network traffic
Topology Visualization	View network architecture

The module emphasized that Network Watcher provides both real-time diagnostics and ongoing monitoring.

Step 4: Understanding IP Flow Verify

A major focus of the module was IP Flow Verify.

IP Flow Verify helps determine whether traffic is allowed or denied by Network Security Groups.

Example:
Source VM
      ↓
Destination VM
      ↓
Port 443

Network Watcher checks:

Source IP
Destination IP
Port number
Protocol
NSG rules
Result:
Allowed

or

Denied
Benefits:
Faster NSG troubleshooting
Security validation
Immediate visibility into traffic decisions

This is one of the most useful troubleshooting tools for Azure Administrators.

Step 5: Understanding Next Hop

The training introduced the Next Hop diagnostic tool.

Next Hop identifies the route Azure uses to send traffic to a destination.

Example:
VM
 ↓
Azure Firewall
 ↓
Internet

Network Watcher shows:

Destination
Route selected
Next hop type
Benefits:
Route troubleshooting
Verification of custom routes
Validation of User-Defined Routes (UDRs)
Key concepts learned:
Azure may choose different routes based on route precedence
Next Hop helps explain traffic behavior
Step 6: Understanding Effective Security Rules

The module covered Effective Security Rules.

Effective Security Rules show the final security policy applied to a network interface.

Sources of rules:
Network Security Groups
Subnet NSGs
NIC-level NSGs
Default security rules
Benefits:
Security troubleshooting
Policy validation
Visibility into final traffic permissions
Key lessons learned:
Multiple NSGs can affect a resource
Effective rules show the final outcome

This is a frequently tested networking concept for AZ-104.

Step 7: Understanding Packet Capture

The training introduced Packet Capture.

Packet Capture collects network traffic directly from Azure virtual machines.

Captured information:
Source IP addresses
Destination IP addresses
Protocols
Ports
Packet contents
Benefits:
Deep troubleshooting
Security investigations
Connectivity diagnostics
Common use cases:
Application failures
Network latency analysis
Security investigations

Packet Capture provides functionality similar to tools like Wireshark but within Azure.

Step 8: Understanding Connection Troubleshoot

